# wtm-gui __VERSION__ (Linux)

This is the wtm desktop app — a native Git worktree manager built with
[GPUI](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/tree/main/crates/gpui) (the UI
framework behind Zed) — packaged as a self-contained tarball for Linux.

**Status:** this build compiles and has been exercised in a headless
container during development, but has not yet been run on real Linux
desktop hardware. If something breaks, please file an issue:
https://github.com/codenameakshay/wtm-manager/issues

## Install

    ./install.sh                  # into ~/.local, no root required
    sudo ./install.sh --system    # into /usr/local, for all users
    ./install.sh --prefix DIR     # into an arbitrary prefix

Run `./install.sh --help` for details. It copies:

    bin/wtm-gui                                             -> <prefix>/bin/wtm-gui
    share/applications/dev.wtm.app.desktop                  -> <prefix>/share/applications/
    share/icons/hicolor/<size>x<size>/apps/dev.wtm.app.png  -> <prefix>/share/icons/hicolor/<size>x<size>/apps/

and refreshes the desktop/icon caches (`update-desktop-database`,
`gtk-update-icon-cache`) if those tools are installed, so the app shows up
in your application launcher with its own icon rather than a generic one.

## Runtime dependencies

wtm-gui is a GPUI app. On Linux it either links directly against, or loads
at startup, these shared libraries (derived by running `ldd` and inspecting
the binary for `dlopen` calls against a release build — not guessed):

  - `libc6`, `libgcc-s1`, `zlib1g` — base runtime, present on essentially
    every Debian/Ubuntu-family system already.
  - `libxcb1`, `libxkbcommon0`, `libxkbcommon-x11-0` — X11 and keyboard
    layout handling.
  - `libvulkan1` — GPU rendering. This is `dlopen`ed at startup rather than
    linked, so `ldd` won't show it, but the app cannot draw a window
    without it and will fail at launch, not at build time.
  - `libwayland-client0` — only exercised under a Wayland session, also
    `dlopen`ed at startup.

These are present on essentially any desktop Linux install; a minimal
server or container base image is the likeliest place to be missing
`libvulkan1` or `libwayland-client0` specifically. Install them with your
distribution's package manager if wtm-gui fails to launch.

## Uninstall

Remove the files `install.sh` copied — `bin/wtm-gui`,
`share/applications/dev.wtm.app.desktop`, and each
`share/icons/hicolor/<size>x<size>/apps/dev.wtm.app.png` — from whichever
prefix you installed into.
